        {
            "id": "33095131",
            "user": "WikiHow Horizon 3",
            "timestamp": "17:21, 25 January 2025",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(15,235 bytes)",
            "change": 6,
            "summary": "(http ► https)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Find shelter immediately. If you find yourself caught in a lightning storm, the key to minimizing danger is to get inside a protective structure. While most people seek shelter if lightning appears to be near, people commonly wait too long to seek shelter. If you can detect lightning, it may be close enough to strike you. Don’t wait for it to strike right next to you (or on top of you) to get to safety. Never stand under a tall or short tree, and avoid being close to power lines as they're both excellent conductors of electricity and could potentially cause death, if not serious injury.<ref>[v161396_b01]. 3 March 2021.</ref> Find shelter near or under a stony shelter such as a cavern. <ref> https://www.ready.gov/thunderstorms-lightning</ref>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Stay away from windows. Keep windows closed, and try to stay within inner rooms of the structure. Windows provide a direct path for the lightning to travel.<ref>https://www.thorntonweather.com/blog/education/indoor-lightning-safety-staying-safe-in-your-home-or-office/</ref>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Don’t touch anything metal or electrical. Using a landline phone is the main cause of lightning-related injuries in the US. Lightning can travel into the home from through any material that conducts electricity. This includes landlines, electrical wiring, and plumbing.<ref>https://www.thorntonweather.com/blog/education/indoor-lightning-safety-staying-safe-in-your-home-or-office/</ref> [[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Remove your backpack. If you are hiking with a metal frame backpack, remove it as soon as you detect lightning. Make sure to leave it at least {{convert|100|ft|m|1}} from wherever you are taking shelter.<ref> https://www.hikingdude.com/hiking-lightning.php</ref>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 8 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Research the local climate: in some areas you can almost guarantee a thunderstorm on summer afternoons. Schedule your activities to avoid many high-risk situations. Those hot, muggy days are just the thing that a thunderstorm needs to get going.<ref> https://www.nws.noaa.gov/</ref>",
                    "#Calculate the distance to the lightning. If conditions permit good visibility, and it’s not practical to seek shelter whenever you notice a strike, use the 30 second rule:  if the time between a lightning flash and the resulting thunder is 30 seconds or less (aka {{convert|6|mi}} or less), get to shelter immediately.<ref>https://www.hikingdude.com/hiking-lightning.php</ref> [[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 14 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Find shelter immediately. If you find yourself caught in a lightning storm, the key to minimizing danger is to get inside a protective structure. While most people seek shelter if lightning appears to be near, people commonly wait too long to seek shelter. If you can detect lightning, it may be close enough to strike you. Don’t wait for it to strike right next to you (or on top of you) to get to safety. Never stand under a tall or short tree, and avoid being close to power lines as they're both excellent conductors of electricity and could potentially cause death, if not serious injury.<ref>[v161396_b01]. 3 March 2021.</ref> Find shelter near or under a stony shelter such as a cavern. <ref> http://www.ready.gov/thunderstorms-lightning</ref>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Stay away from windows. Keep windows closed, and try to stay within inner rooms of the structure. Windows provide a direct path for the lightning to travel.<ref>http://www.thorntonweather.com/blog/education/indoor-lightning-safety-staying-safe-in-your-home-or-office/</ref>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Don’t touch anything metal or electrical. Using a landline phone is the main cause of lightning-related injuries in the US. Lightning can travel into the home from through any material that conducts electricity. This includes landlines, electrical wiring, and plumbing.<ref>http://www.thorntonweather.com/blog/education/indoor-lightning-safety-staying-safe-in-your-home-or-office/</ref> [[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Remove your backpack. If you are hiking with a metal frame backpack, remove it as soon as you detect lightning. Make sure to leave it at least {{convert|100|ft|m|1}} from wherever you are taking shelter.<ref> http://www.hikingdude.com/hiking-lightning.php</ref>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 8 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Research the local climate: in some areas you can almost guarantee a thunderstorm on summer afternoons. Schedule your activities to avoid many high-risk situations. Those hot, muggy days are just the thing that a thunderstorm needs to get going.<ref> http://www.nws.noaa.gov/</ref>",
                    "#Calculate the distance to the lightning. If conditions permit good visibility, and it’s not practical to seek shelter whenever you notice a strike, use the 30 second rule:  if the time between a lightning flash and the resulting thunder is 30 seconds or less (aka {{convert|6|mi}} or less), get to shelter immediately.<ref>http://www.hikingdude.com/hiking-lightning.php</ref> [[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 14 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
