        {
            "id": "26665608",
            "user": "Seymour Edits",
            "timestamp": "00:18, 11 June 2019",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(14,883 bytes)",
            "change": 909,
            "summary": "(Added sources for the wikihaus content team.)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "sourcing",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Stay away from windows. Keep windows closed, and try to stay within inner rooms of the structure. Windows provide a direct path for the lightning to travel.<ref>http://www.thorntonweather.com/blog/education/indoor-lightning-safety-staying-safe-in-your-home-or-office/</ref>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Don’t touch anything metal or electrical. Using a landline phone is the main cause of lightning-related injuries in the US. Lightning can travel into the home from through any material that conducts electricity. This includes landlines, electrical wiring, and plumbing.<ref>http://www.thorntonweather.com/blog/education/indoor-lightning-safety-staying-safe-in-your-home-or-office/</ref> [[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Minimize your risk. If you absolutely cannot reach shelter during a lightning storm, do everything you can to reduce your risk of being struck by lightning.<ref>https://www.accuweather.com/en/weather-news/7-lightning-safety-tips-if-youre-caught-outside-during-a-thunderstorm/70002014</ref>  [[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Get out of the water. If you are fishing or swimming, get out of the water immediately, and move away from the body of water. Being near water is extremely dangerous during a lightning storm.<ref>https://www.accuweather.com/en/weather-news/7-lightning-safety-tips-if-youre-caught-outside-during-a-thunderstorm/70002014</ref> [[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Spread out.<ref>https://www.cdc.gov/disasters/lightning/safetytips.html</ref>  If you are caught in a lightning storm with a group of people, maintain a distance of at least {{convert|50|-|100|ft|m|1}} between each person. This will reduce the risk of lightning traveling from one person to another.[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 7 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Assume the “lightning crouch”. Squat down with your feet together, your head tucked to your chest or between your knees, and your hands covering your ears or flat against your knees. Do not lie flat on the ground, as this gives the lightning a larger target.<ref>https://www.cdc.gov/disasters/lightning/safetytips.html</ref> [[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Plan ahead. The best way to avoid injury from a lightning storm is to avoid it completely. Make your plans with dangerous weather in mind. Listen to the local weather forecast, and pay special attention to thunderstorm advisories.<ref>https://www.cdc.gov/disasters/lightning/safetytips.html</ref> [[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 12 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Calculate the distance to the lightning. If conditions permit good visibility, and it’s not practical to seek shelter whenever you notice a strike, use the 30 second rule:  if the time between a lightning flash and the resulting thunder is 30 seconds or less (aka {{convert|6|mi}} or less), get to shelter immediately.<ref>https://www.cdc.gov/disasters/lightning/safetytips.html</ref> [[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 14 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Call emergency services. Because lightning strikes can cause cardiac arrest, aggressive resuscitation may be necessary. If you cannot dial 9-1-1, designate someone else to.<ref>https://www.redcross.org/get-help/how-to-prepare-for-emergencies/types-of-emergencies/thunderstorm.html</ref> [[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 18 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Start CPR. People struck by lightning do not retain an electrical charge, so you can immediately touch them and begin treatment.<ref>https://www.redcross.org/get-help/how-to-prepare-for-emergencies/types-of-emergencies/thunderstorm.html</ref>  Do not remove the burned clothes unless absolutely necessary.[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 20 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Stay away from windows. Keep windows closed, and try to stay within inner rooms of the structure. Windows provide a direct path for the lightning to travel.[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Don’t touch anything metal or electrical. Using a landline phone is the main cause of lightning-related injuries in the US. Lightning can travel into the home from through any material that conducts electricity. This includes landlines, electrical wiring, and plumbing.[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Minimize your risk. If you absolutely cannot reach shelter during a lightning storm, do everything you can to reduce your risk of being struck by lightning. [[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Get out of the water. If you are fishing or swimming, get out of the water immediately, and move away from the body of water. Being near water is extremely dangerous during a lightning storm.[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Spread out. If you are caught in a lightning storm with a group of people, maintain a distance of at least {{convert|50|-|100|ft|m|1}} between each person. This will reduce the risk of lightning traveling from one person to another.[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 7 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Assume the “lightning crouch”. Squat down with your feet together, your head tucked to your chest or between your knees, and your hands covering your ears or flat against your knees. Do not lie flat on the ground, as this gives the lightning a larger target.[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Plan ahead. The best way to avoid injury from a lightning storm is to avoid it completely. Make your plans with dangerous weather in mind. Listen to the local weather forecast, and pay special attention to thunderstorm advisories.[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 12 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Calculate the distance to the lightning. If conditions permit good visibility, and it’s not practical to seek shelter whenever you notice a strike, use the 30 second rule:  if the time between a lightning flash and the resulting thunder is 30 seconds or less (aka {{convert|6|mi}} or less), get to shelter immediately.[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 14 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Call emergency services. Because lightning strikes can cause cardiac arrest, aggressive resuscitation may be necessary. If you cannot dial 9-1-1, designate someone else to.[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 18 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Start CPR. People struck by lightning do not retain an electrical charge, so you can immediately touch them and begin treatment. Do not remove the burned clothes unless absolutely necessary.[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 20 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "* If you are located in a house, you could go in a basement to protect yourself from trees."
                ]
            }
        },
