 taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco taco''' '''Place bold text here'''  beautiful and inspiring phenomenon, but it can be deadly. Over the past 30 years, lightning has killed an average of 67 people per year in the United States alone. Fortunately, most lightning-related deaths are preventable. Follow these steps to safety the next time there’s fire in the sky."
                ],
