        {
            "id": "21145545",
            "user": "WikiHow Projects",
            "timestamp": "12:43, 15 October 2016",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(13,017 bytes)",
            "change": 8,
            "summary": "(→‎Steps:  Added images)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Find shelter immediately. If you find yourself caught in a lightning storm, the key to minimizing danger is to get inside a protective structure. While most people seek shelter if lightning appears to be near, people commonly wait too long to seek shelter. If you can detect lightning, it may be close enough to strike you. Don’t wait for it to strike right next to you (or on top of you) to get to safety. Never stand under a tall or short tree, and avoid being close to power lines as they're both excellent conductors of electricity and could potentially cause death, if not serious injury. Find shelter near or under a stony shelter , say a cavern or something . <ref> http://www.ready.gov/thunderstorms-lightning</ref>[[Image:Overcome-the-Fear-of-Thunder-Step-8.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Stay away from windows. Keep windows closed, and try to stay within inner rooms of the structure. Windows provide a direct path for the lightning to travel.[[Image:Calm-a-Rabbit-During-a-Thunderstorm-Step-4-Version-2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Don’t touch anything metal or electrical. Using a landline phone is the main cause of lightning-related injuries in the US. Lightning can travel into the home from through any material that conducts electricity. This includes landlines, electrical wiring, and plumbing.[[Image:Do-Electrical-Testing-Step-3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Stay inside. Stay inside at least 30 minutes after the last strike. Don’t go out just because the rain is starting to let up. There is still a significant risk of lightning strikes from a departing storm.[[Image:Help-a-Dog-Overcome-a-Fear-of-Fireworks-or-Thunder-Step-7.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Minimize your risk. If you absolutely cannot reach shelter during a lightning storm, do everything you can to minimize your risk.[[Image:Photograph Lightning Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Get out of the water. If you are fishing or swimming, get out of the water immediately, and move away from the body of water. Being near water is extremely dangerous during a lightning storm.[[Image:Survive-a-Tsunami-(for-Kids)-Step-6.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Spread out. If you are caught in a lightning storm with a group of people, maintain a distance of at least {{convert|50|-|100|ft|m|1}} between each person. This will reduce the risk of lightning traveling from one person to another.[[Image:Accept-Being-Shy-Step-9-Version-2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Assume the “lightning crouch”. Squat down with your feet together, your head tucked to your chest or between your knees, and your hands covering your ears or flat against your knees. Do not lie flat on the ground, as this gives the lightning a larger target.[[Image:Handle-an-Emergency-Situation-Step-6.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Watch the skies. When you’re out and about, watch the sky for signs of approaching thunderstorms, such as rain, darkening skies, or towering cumulonimbus clouds. If you can anticipate lightning before the first strike, you can avoid being caught in a bad situation.[[Image:Survive-Being-Trapped-in-Your-Car-During-a-Snowstorm-Step-8.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Calculate the distance to the lightning. If conditions permit good visibility, and it’s not practical to seek shelter whenever you notice a strike, use the 30 second rule:  if the time between a lightning flash and the resulting thunder is 30 seconds or less (aka {{convert|6|mi}} or less), get to shelter immediately. [[Image:Overcome-the-Fear-of-Thunder-Step-2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Plan your response. If you are in an area that you expect will see lightning storms, know where safe shelters are. Communicate your plans to your group so that everyone knows what to do in an emergency.[[Image:Storm-Chase-Step-2.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Find shelter immediately. If you find yourself caught in a lightning storm, the key to minimizing danger is to get inside a protective structure. While most people seek shelter if lightning appears to be near, people commonly wait too long to seek shelter. If you can detect lightning, it may be close enough to strike you. Don’t wait for it to strike right next to you (or on top of you) to get to safety. Never stand under a tall or short tree, and avoid being close to power lines as they're both excellent conductors of electricity and could potentially cause death, if not serious injury. Find shelter near or under a stony shelter , say a cavern or something . <ref> http://www.ready.gov/thunderstorms-lightning</ref>\t[[Image:Calm a Dog During Thunderstorms Step 1 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Stay away from windows. Keep windows closed, and try to stay within inner rooms of the structure. Windows provide a direct path for the lightning to travel.[[Image:Calm a Dog During Thunderstorms Step 6.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Don’t touch anything metal or electrical. Using a landline phone is the main cause of lightning-related injuries in the US. Lightning can travel into the home from through any material that conducts electricity. This includes landlines, electrical wiring, and plumbing.[[Image:Prepare for a Thunderstorm Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Stay inside. Stay inside at least 30 minutes after the last strike. Don’t go out just because the rain is starting to let up. There is still a significant risk of lightning strikes from a departing storm.[[Image:Overcome the Fear of Thunder Step 8.jpg|center]]",
                    "",
                    "#Minimize your risk. If you absolutely cannot reach shelter during a lightning storm, do everything you can to minimize your risk.[[Image:Climb a Tree Step 2 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Get out of the water. If you are fishing or swimming, get out of the water immediately, and move away from the body of water. Being near water is extremely dangerous during a lightning storm.[[Image:Paddle Board Step 1.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Spread out. If you are caught in a lightning storm with a group of people, maintain a distance of at least {{convert|50|-|100|ft|m|1}} between each person. This will reduce the risk of lightning traveling from one person to another.[[Image:Make People Love You Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Assume the “lightning crouch”. Squat down with your feet together, your head tucked to your chest or between your knees, and your hands covering your ears or flat against your knees. Do not lie flat on the ground, as this gives the lightning a larger target.[[Image:Prepare for a Thunderstorm Step 12.jpg|center]]",
                    "# Watch the skies. When you’re out and about, watch the sky for signs of approaching thunderstorms, such as rain, darkening skies, or towering cumulonimbus clouds. If you can anticipate lightning before the first strike, you can avoid being caught in a bad situation.[[Image:Find an Iridium Flare Step 6.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Calculate the distance to the lightning. If conditions permit good visibility, and it’s not practical to seek shelter whenever you notice a strike, use the 30 second rule:  if the time between a lightning flash and the resulting thunder is 30 seconds or less (aka {{convert|6|mi}} or less), get to shelter immediately. [[Image:Avoid Being Struck by Lightning When Caught Unawares Step 6.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Plan your response. If you are in an area that you expect will see lightning storms, know where safe shelters are. Communicate your plans to your group so that everyone knows what to do in an emergency. [[Image:Overcome the Fear of Thunder Step 2.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
