            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "reverted_by": "WikiHowUser20230227233914",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Spread out. If you are caught in a lightning storm with a group of people, maintain a distance of at least {{convert|50|-|100|ft|m|1}} between each person. This will reduce the risk of lightning traveling from one person to another.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 7.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Remove your backpack. If you are hiking with a metal frame backpack, remove it as soon as you detect lightning. Make sure to leave it at least {{convert|100|ft|m|1}} from wherever you are taking shelter.<ref> http://www.hikingdude.com/hiking-lightning.php</ref><br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 8.jpg|center|550px]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Spread out. If you are caught in a lightning storm with a group of people, maintain a distance of at least 50-100 feet between each person. This will reduce the risk of lightning traveling from one person to another.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 7.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Remove your backpack. If you are hiking with a metal frame backpack, remove it as soon as you detect lightning. Make sure to leave it at least 100 feet from wherever you are taking shelter.<ref> http://www.hikingdude.com/hiking-lightning.php</ref><br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 8.jpg|center|550px]]"
                ]
            }
        },
