        {
            "id": "13628351",
            "user": "168.184.14.3",
            "timestamp": "16:02, 16 May 2014",
            "anon": true,
            "size": "(13,735 bytes)",
            "change": 59,
            "summary": "",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "reverted_by": "VandaAnda",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "===Taking Shelter and Staying Safe from people===",
                    "#Find shelter immediately. If you find yourself caught in a lightning storm, the key to minimizing danger is to get inside a protective structure. While most people seek shelter if lightning appears to be near, people commonly wait too long to seek shelter. If you can detect lightning, it may be close enough to strike you. Don’t wait for it to strike right next to you (or on top of you) to get to safety. Never stand under a tall or short tree, and avoid being close to power lines as they're both excellent conductors of electricity and could potentially cause death, if not serious injury. Find shelter near or under a stony shelter , say a cavern or something . <ref> http://www.ready.gov/thunderstorms-lightning</ref><br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "===Surviving Thunderstorms Outside make sure you take an umbrella===",
                    "#Install a lightning rod make sure you catch enough fish. If you live in a lightning-prone area, installing a lightning rod can help protect your family and your property.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 17.jpg|center|550px]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "===Taking Shelter and Staying Safe===",
                    "#Find shelter immediately. If you find yourself caught in a lightning storm, the key to minimizing danger is to get inside a protective structure. While most people seek shelter if lightning appears to be near, people commonly wait too long to seek shelter. If yhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhhou can detect lightning, it may be close enough to strike you. Don’t wait for it to strike right next to you (or on top of you) to get to safety. Never stand under a tall or short tree, and avoid being close to power lines as they're both excellent conductors of electricity and could potentially cause death, if not serious injury. Find shelter near or under a stony shelter , say a cavern or something . <ref> http://www.ready.gov/thunderstorms-lightning</ref><br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "===Surviving Thunderstorms Outside===",
                    "#Install a lightning rod. If you live in a lightning-prone area, installing a lightning rod can help protect your family and your property.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 17.jpg|center|550px]]"
                ]
            }
        },
