        {
            "id": "13570629",
            "user": "RockyRaccoon",
            "timestamp": "21:15, 10 May 2014",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(13,660 bytes)",
            "change": 6470,
            "summary": "(Reverted edits by 90.195.10.78 (Talk); changed back to last version by Votebot)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "revert",
            "reverted_by": "VandaAnda",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "{{fa}}Lightning is a beautiful and inspiring phenomenon, but it can be deadly. Over the past 30 years, lightning has killed an average of 67 people per year in the United States alone. Fortunately, most lightning-related deaths are preventable. Follow these steps to safety the next time there’s fire in the sky.",
                    "[[Category:Storms]]",
                    "[[Category:Weather Safety]]",
                    "== Steps ==",
                    "===Taking Shelter and Staying Safe===",
                    "#Stay inside. Stay inside at least 30 minutes after the last strike. Don’t go out if the rain starts letting up. There is still a significant risk of lightning strikes from a departing storm.<ref> http://www.lightningsafety.noaa.gov/overview.htm</ref><br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 4.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "===Surviving Thunderstorms Outside===",
                    "#Minimize your risk. If you absolutely cannot reach shelter during a lightning storm, do everything you can to minimize your risk.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 5.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#*Move to a lower elevation. Lightning is much more likely to strike objects at higher elevations. Do what you can do get as low as possible.",
                    "#*Avoid large open spaces where you are taller than anything else around you, like a golf course or soccer field.",
                    "#*This is a difficult position to hold and it by no means guarantees your safety. However, by making it easier for a lightning strike to flow over your body rather than through vital organs, you may be able to sustain a smaller injury from it.",
                    "#Be alert for an imminent lightning strike. If lightning is about to strike you or strike near you, your hair may stand on end, or you may feel a tingling in your skin. Light metal objects may vibrate, and you may hear a crackling sound or \"kee kee\" sound. If you detect any of these signals, assume the lightning crouch immediately.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 10.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Plan your response. If you are in an area that you expect will see lightning storms, know where safe shelters are. Communicate your plans to your group so that everyone knows what to do in an emergency.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 15.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#[[ Build Your Own Emergency Disaster Kit |Prepare an emergency kit]]. Be ready with first aid and other disaster essentials. You may lose power during a thunderstorm, so have alternative light sources available.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 16.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Install a lightning rod. If you live in a lightning-prone area, installing a lightning rod can help protect your family and your property.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 17.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#*Have your lightning rod professionally installed. An incorrectly installed rod can increase the chance of a lightning strike.",
                    "===Treating Lightning Strike Victims===",
                    "#Call emergency services. Because lightning strikes can cause cardiac arrest, aggressive resuscitation may be necessary. If you cannot dial 9-1-1, designate someone else to.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 18.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Make sure it is safe to help. Do not put yourself in danger trying to help a lightning strike victim. Either wait until the immediate danger has passed, or move the victim to a safer location.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 19.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#*Despite the common myth, lightning can strike the same place twice.",
                    "#Start CPR. People struck by lightning do not retain an electrical charge, so you can immediately touch them and begin treatment. Do not remove the burned clothes unless absolutely necessary.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 20.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#*[[Do CPR on a Child|Perform Child CPR]] if the victim is a child.",
                    "#*[[Do CPR on an Adult|Perform Adult CPR]] on adult victims.",
                    "#Treat the victim for shock. Lay the victim down on his or her back with the head resting slightly lower than the torso. Elevate and support the legs.<ref> http://firstaid.webmd.com/lightning-strike-treatment</ref>",
                    "",
                    "",
                    "== Tips ==",
                    "*Small boats are dangerous places to be in a thunderstorm.  If you can’t get to shore, however, do not enter the water — stay in the boat, even if it’s an open sailboat with a mast. There is a mistaken belief that being in the water is safer, but lightning can just as easily strike water (or the electrical charge can be carried through water), and if you’re struck and rendered unconscious, you don’t want to be in the water.",
                    "*Stay away from windows in case of one breaks from the wind.",
                    "*Commercial lightning detection devices and weather warning services are available and should be considered for use by golf courses, parks, etc.",
                    "*Lightning can travel several feet through the ground, so distance yourself from tall, isolated objects.  By the same reasoning, be aware that a person may have been hit by lightning, even if you didn’t see the lightning hit the person.",
                    "*If a thunderstorm is approaching, protect electronics and electric appliances by unplugging them in advance. Don't use corded telephones, as lightning can travel through the cords. Do not unplug anything during a lightning storm, only in advance.",
                    "*When adopting the lightning crouch, protect your ears. The thunder is dangerously loud.",
                    "*Lightning does not only happen during thunderstorms; it can also happen during volcanic eruptions. So you should also know about volcano safety and lightning safety. The more ash there is the more likely lightning will strike.",
                    "*Wearing portable electronics with headphones during a lightning storm can increase the likelihood of severe injury in the case of a strike - not only to the ears, but to anywhere on the body that the headphone cables lay against.",
                    "*Lightning is common in the summer throughout much of the United States. Florida receives the most lightning strikes per square mile per year.",
                    "*Wear as much as rubber as possible. Rubber is a bad conductor and lightning will often bounce off it or sink into it. Also avoid touching metal as lightning travels around metal and touching it will make it travel to you.",
                    "",
                    "== Warnings ==",
                    "*When seeking lower ground, try to choose an area that is safe from flooding.",
                    "*Do not attempt to watch a storm through an open window or door or from a porch.  Unenclosed areas are not safe, even if in a suitable shelter.",
                    "*Severe thunderstorms can (and sometimes do) produce tornadoes with little or no warning. Keep alert for potentially life-threatening weather if the storm(s) in the area are particularly strong. Stay alert even if there were no thunderstorm warnings issued.",
                    "",
                    "== Related wikiHows ==",
                    "*[[Protect Trees from Lightning|How to Protect Trees from Lightning]]",
                    "*[[Calculate the Distance from Lightning|How to Calculate the Distance from Lightning]]",
                    "*[[Prepare for a Tornado|How to Prepare for a Tornado]]",
                    "*[[Prepare for a Wildfire|How to Prepare for a Wildfire]]",
                    "*[[Be Safe During a Heat Emergency|How to Be Safe During a Heat Emergency]]",
                    "",
                    "== Sources and Citations ==",
                    "{{reflist}}",
                    "[[es:protegerse en una tormenta eléctrica]]",
                    "[[it:Proteggersi da una Tempesta]]",
                    "[[de:Suche Schutz vor einem Gewitter]]",
                    "[[fr:se protéger d'un orage]]",
                    "[[pt:Se Proteger em uma Tempestade de Raios]]",
                    "[[ru:защитить себя от молнии]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "",
                    "#Stay inside. Stay inside at least 30 minutes after the last strike. Don’t go out if the rain starts letting up. There is still a significant risk of lightning strikes from a departing storm.<ref> http://www.lightningsafety.noaa.gov/overview.htm</ref><br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 4.jpg|centereverything you can course or soccer field.",
                    "#*This is a difficult position to hold and it by no means guarantees your safety. However, by making it easier for a lightning strike to er your body rather than through vital organs, you may be able to sustain a smaller injury from it.",
                    "#Be alert for an imminent lightning you",
                    "#Plan your response. If you are in an area that you expect will see lightning storms, know where safe shelters are. Communicate your plans to your group so that"
                ]
            }
        },
