        {
            "id": "10903802",
            "user": "Wikiphoto",
            "timestamp": "21:48, 23 August 2013",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(13,219 bytes)",
            "change": 179,
            "summary": "(Enlarging and centering Steps photos)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Find shelter immediately. If you find yourself caught in a lightning storm, the key to minimizing danger is to get inside a protective structure. While most people seek shelter if lightning appears to be near, people commonly wait too long to seek shelter. If you can detect lightning, it may be close enough to strike you. Don’t wait for it to strike right next to you (or on top of you) to get to safety.<ref> http://www.ready.gov/thunderstorms-lightning</ref><br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 1.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Stay away from windows. Keep windows closed, and try to stay within inner rooms of the structure. Windows provide a direct path for the lightning to travel.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 2.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Don’t touch anything metal or electrical. Using a landline phone is the main cause of lightning-related injuries in the US.<ref> http://www.weather.com/life/safety/thunderstorm/article/thunderstorms-indoor-lightning-safety_2011-10-07</ref> Lightning can travel into the home from through any material that conducts electricity. This includes landlines, electrical wiring, and plumbing.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 3.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Stay inside. Stay inside at least 30 minutes after the last strike. Don’t go out if the rain starts letting up. There is still a significant risk of lightning strikes from a departing storm.<ref> http://www.lightningsafety.noaa.gov/overview.htm</ref><br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 4.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Minimize your risk. If you absolutely cannot reach shelter during a lightning storm, do everything you can to minimize your risk.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 5.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Get out of the water. If you are fishing or swimming, get out of the water immediately, and move away from the body of water. Being near water is extremely dangerous during a lightning storm.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 6.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Spread out. If you are caught in a lightning storm with a group of people, maintain a distance of at least 50-100 feet between each person. This will reduce the risk of lightning traveling from one person to another.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 7.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Remove your backpack. If you are hiking with a metal frame backpack, remove it as soon as you detect lightning. Make sure to leave it at least 100 feet from wherever you are taking shelter.<ref> http://www.hikingdude.com/hiking-lightning.php</ref><br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 8.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Assume the “lightning crouch”.<ref>http://www.edu4hazards.org/lightning_crouch.html</ref> Squat down with your feet together, your head tucked to your chest or between your knees, and your hands covering your ears or flat against your knees. Do NOT lie flat on the ground, as this gives the lightning a larger target.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 9.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Be alert for an imminent lightning strike. If lightning is about to strike you or strike near you, your hair may stand on end, or you may feel a tingling in your skin. Light metal objects may vibrate, and you may hear a crackling sound or \"kee kee\" sound. If you detect any of these signals, assume the lightning crouch immediately.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 10.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Wear wellington boots. They are made of rubber which is a bad electrical conductor.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 11.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Plan ahead. The best way to avoid injury from a lightning storm is to avoid it completely. Make your plans with dangerous weather in mind. Listen to the local weather forecast, and pay special attention to thunderstorm advisories.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 12.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "# Watch the skies. When you’re out and about, watch the sky for signs of approaching thunderstorms, such as rain, darkening skies, or towering cumulonimbus clouds. If you can anticipate lightning before the first strike, you can avoid being caught in a bad situation.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 13.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Calculate the distance to the lightning. If conditions permit good visibility, and it’s not practical to seek shelter whenever you notice a strike, use the 30 second rule:  if the time between a lightning flash and the resulting thunder is 30 seconds or less (aka 6 miles or less), get to shelter immediately.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 14.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Plan your response. If you are in an area that you expect will see lightning storms, know where safe shelters are. Communicate your plans to your group so that everyone knows what to do in an emergency.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 15.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#[[ Build Your Own Emergency Disaster Kit |Prepare an emergency kit]]. Be ready with first aid and other disaster essentials. You may lose power during a thunderstorm, so have alternative light sources available.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 16.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Install a lightning rod. If you live in a lightning-prone area, installing a lightning rod can help protect your family and your property.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 17.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Call emergency services. Because lightning strikes can cause cardiac arrest, aggressive resuscitation may be necessary. If you cannot dial 9-1-1, designate someone else to.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 18.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Make sure it is safe to help. Do not put yourself in danger trying to help a lightning strike victim. Either wait until the immediate danger has passed, or move the victim to a safer location.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 19.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    "#Start CPR. People struck by lightning do not retain an electrical charge, so you can immediately touch them and begin treatment. Do not remove the burned clothes unless absolutely necessary.<br><br>[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 20.jpg|center|550px]]",
                    ""
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 1.jpg|right|300px]]Find shelter immediately. If you find yourself caught in a lightning storm, the key to minimizing danger is to get inside a protective structure. While most people seek shelter if lightning appears to be near, people commonly wait too long to seek shelter. If you can detect lightning, it may be close enough to strike you. Don’t wait for it to strike right next to you (or on top of you) to get to safety.<ref> http://www.ready.gov/thunderstorms-lightning</ref>",
                    "#[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 2.jpg|right|300px]]Stay away from windows. Keep windows closed, and try to stay within inner rooms of the structure. Windows provide a direct path for the lightning to travel.",
                    "#[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 3.jpg|right|300px]]Don’t touch anything metal or electrical. Using a landline phone is the main cause of lightning-related injuries in the US.<ref> http://www.weather.com/life/safety/thunderstorm/article/thunderstorms-indoor-lightning-safety_2011-10-07</ref> Lightning can travel into the home from through any material that conducts electricity. This includes landlines, electrical wiring, and plumbing.",
                    "#[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 4.jpg|right|300px]]Stay inside. Stay inside at least 30 minutes after the last strike. Don’t go out if the rain starts letting up. There is still a significant risk of lightning strikes from a departing storm.<ref> http://www.lightningsafety.noaa.gov/overview.htm</ref>",
                    "#[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 5.jpg|right|300px]]Minimize your risk. If you absolutely cannot reach shelter during a lightning storm, do everything you can to minimize your risk.",
                    "#[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 6.jpg|right|300px]]Get out of the water. If you are fishing or swimming, get out of the water immediately, and move away from the body of water. Being near water is extremely dangerous during a lightning storm.",
                    "#[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 7.jpg|right|300px]]Spread out. If you are caught in a lightning storm with a group of people, maintain a distance of at least 50-100 feet between each person. This will reduce the risk of lightning traveling from one person to another.",
                    "#[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 8.jpg|right|300px]]Remove your backpack. If you are hiking with a metal frame backpack, remove it as soon as you detect lightning. Make sure to leave it at least 100 feet from wherever you are taking shelter.<ref> http://www.hikingdude.com/hiking-lightning.php</ref>",
                    "#[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 9.jpg|right|300px]]Assume the “lightning crouch”.<ref>http://www.edu4hazards.org/lightning_crouch.html</ref> Squat down with your feet together, your head tucked to your chest or between your knees, and your hands covering your ears or flat against your knees. Do NOT lie flat on the ground, as this gives the lightning a larger target.",
                    "#[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 10.jpg|right|300px]]Be alert for an imminent lightning strike. If lightning is about to strike you or strike near you, your hair may stand on end, or you may feel a tingling in your skin. Light metal objects may vibrate, and you may hear a crackling sound or \"kee kee\" sound. If you detect any of these signals, assume the lightning crouch immediately.",
                    "#[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 11.jpg|right|300px]]Wear wellington boots. They are made of rubber which is a bad electrical conductor.",
                    "#[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 12.jpg|right|300px]]Plan ahead. The best way to avoid injury from a lightning storm is to avoid it completely. Make your plans with dangerous weather in mind. Listen to the local weather forecast, and pay special attention to thunderstorm advisories.",
                    "# [[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 13.jpg|right|300px]]Watch the skies. When you’re out and about, watch the sky for signs of approaching thunderstorms, such as rain, darkening skies, or towering cumulonimbus clouds. If you can anticipate lightning before the first strike, you can avoid being caught in a bad situation.",
                    "#[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 14.jpg|right|300px]]Calculate the distance to the lightning. If conditions permit good visibility, and it’s not practical to seek shelter whenever you notice a strike, use the 30 second rule:  if the time between a lightning flash and the resulting thunder is 30 seconds or less (aka 6 miles or less), get to shelter immediately.",
                    "#[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 15.jpg|right|300px]]Plan your response. If you are in an area that you expect will see lightning storms, know where safe shelters are. Communicate your plans to your group so that everyone knows what to do in an emergency.",
                    "#[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 16.jpg|right|300px]][[ Build Your Own Emergency Disaster Kit |Prepare an emergency kit]]. Be ready with first aid and other disaster essentials. You may lose power during a thunderstorm, so have alternative light sources available.",
                    "#[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 17.jpg|right|300px]]Install a lightning rod. If you live in a lightning-prone area, installing a lightning rod can help protect your family and your property.",
                    "#[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 18.jpg|right|300px]]Call emergency services. Because lightning strikes can cause cardiac arrest, aggressive resuscitation may be necessary. If you cannot dial 9-1-1, designate someone else to.",
                    "#[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 19.jpg|right|300px]]Make sure it is safe to help. Do not put yourself in danger trying to help a lightning strike victim. Either wait until the immediate danger has passed, or move the victim to a safer location.",
                    "#[[Image:Protect Yourself in a Thunderstorm Step 20.jpg|right|300px]]Start CPR. People struck by lightning do not retain an electrical charge, so you can immediately touch them and begin treatment. Do not remove the burned clothes unless absolutely necessary."
                ]
            }
        },
