                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#[[Image:173803899_6ee6a89e79.jpg|200px|right]] '''Understand what constitutes suitable shelter.''' The key to minimizing lightning danger is to get inside a protective structure. Substantial, frequently inhabited buildings (those grounded with plumbing, electrical systems bitch, and, if possible, lightning rods) are best.  Small structures, such as stand alone public restrooms, are generally not good choices, and standing under a tree is a very bad choice.  If you can’t find a substantial structure, get in a car with a metal roof and sides:  if the car is struck, the metal body will conduct the electricity around you, not through you.  When you’re outside, determine where the nearest suitable structure is in advance so that you’ll be ready should you be caught in a lightning storm.."
                ]
            }
        },
