        {
            "id": "4944563",
            "user": "Chris H",
            "timestamp": "18:49, 17 August 2010",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(9,335 bytes)",
            "change": -1064,
            "summary": "(CE)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "major_deletion",
            "reverted_by": "WikiHowUser20221012045502",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#'''Stay safe inside a structure.''' Once in a suitable shelter, you still need to take some extra precautions.  Do not touch land-line phones, plugged-in electrical appliances, or door knobs; keep your distance from electrical or plumbing fixtures, electrical sockets, windows, and doors.  Keep windows closed (this is especially important in a vehicle), and try to stay within inner rooms of the structure.  Stay out of the bathtub or shower, and avoid indoor swimming pools.  In a car, try to avoid touching any part of the metal frame or the car's glass.  In short, stay away from anything that may directly conduct electricity in the event of a strike.",
                    "#'''Treat a lightning victim.'''  If someone is struck by lightning, call the emergency services immediately.  Determine if his or her heart is beating and if he or she is breathing.  [[Do CPR on an Adult|Proceed with CPR]] or mouth-to-mouth resuscitation if warranted.  If the person is still exposed to possible lightning strike (yes, lightning can and does strike the same place twice) see if you can safely move the person.  People struck by lightning do not retain an electrical charge, so do not be afraid of treating a victim.",
                    "#'''If there is absolutely no shelter, squat on the ground, with as little parts as possible touching the ground, and your feet close together.''' Do not lie down as this will provide a bigger target for the lightning to strike.",
                    "*Since lightning can and does blow out power systems, purchase and use self-powered radios flashlights to use in case of a power failure.",
                    "*[[Prepare for a Tornado|How to Prepare for a Tornado]]",
                    "*[[Prepare for a Wildfire|How to Prepare for a Wildfire]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#[[Image:Light_ning_983.jpg|thumb|description]]'''Stay safe inside a structure.'''",
                    "#Once in a suitable shelter, you still need to take some extra precautions.  Do not touch land-line phones, plugged-in electrical appliances, or door knobs; keep your distance from electrical or plumbing fixtures, electrical sockets, windows, and doors.  Keep windows closed (this is especially important in a vehicle), and try to stay within inner rooms of the structure.  Stay out of the bathtub or shower, and avoid indoor swimming pools.  In a car, try to avoid touching any part of the metal frame or the car's glass.  In short, stay away from anything that may directly conduct electricity in the event of a strike.",
                    "#[[Image:171031395_5877213677.jpg|250px|right]] '''Treat a lightning victim.'''  If someone is struck by lightning, call the emergency services immediately.  Determine if his or her heart is beating and if he or she is breathing.  Proceed with CPR or mouth-to-mouth resuscitation if warranted.  If the person is still exposed to possible lightning strike (yes, lightning can and does strike the same place twice) see if you can safely move the person to safety.  People struck by lightning do not retain an electrical charge, so do not be afraid of treating a victim.  If the victim has no pulse and if there is an automated external defibrillator (AED) available (many public places like sports arenas, grocery stores, etc. have them), send a bystander to retrieve it.  Continue CPR while the AED is en route.  Once it arrives, attach the AED and follow the voice prompts.",
                    "#'''If there is absolutely no shelter, squat on the ground, with as little parts as possible touching the ground, and your feet close together. Do not lie down as this will provide a bigger target for the lightning to strike. However, if the victim has no pulse, the AED will be rendered useless, as it uses electric pulses to correct irregular heartbeats only.",
                    "*Since lightning can and does blow out power systems, purchase and use \"Self Powered Radios\" and \"Self Powered Flashlights\", light sticks, so that you have SAFE light and info. in case of a power failure.",
                    "*Avoid operating machinery, i.e. lawn-mowers, edgers, etc.",
                    "*Commercial lightning-detection devices should only be used as a backup to your own senses, such as in low visibility or high noise conditions.  Regardless of the readings you may receive on such a device.",
                    "*[[Climb a 14er|How to Climb a 14er]]",
                    "*[[Survive in the Woods for 3 Days|How to Survive in the Woods for 3 Days]]",
                    "*[[Plan a Backpacking Trip|How to Plan a Backpacking Trip]]",
                    "*[[Survive a Living Hell|How to Survive a Living Hell]]",
                    "*[[\"Magically\" Guess Someone's Favorite Color|How to \"Magically\" Guess Someone's Favorite Color]]"
                ]
            }
        },
