            "id": "2175523",
            "user": "Melwade",
            "timestamp": "09:54, 14 June 2008",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(9,845 bytes)",
            "change": -19,
            "summary": "(Reverted edits by 86.5.222.124 (Talk); changed back to last version by OhioMike)",
            "is_minor": true,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "reverted",
            "contribution_type": "revert",
            "reverted_by": "OhioMike",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#[[Image:171031395_5877213677.jpg|250px|right]] '''Treat a lightning victim.'''  If someone is struck by lightning, call the emergency services immediately.  Determine if his or her heart is beating and if he or she is breathing.  Proceed with CPR or mouth-to-mouth resuscitation if warranted.  If the person is still exposed to possible lightning strike (yes, lightning can and does strike the same place twice) see if you can safely move the person to safety.  People struck by lightning do not retain an electrical charge, so do not be afraid of treating a victim.  If the victim has no pulse and if there is an automated external defibrillator (AED) available (many public places like sports arenas, grocery stores, etc. have them), send a bystander to retrieve it.  Continue CPR while the AED is en route.  Once it arrives, attach the AED and follow the voice prompts."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#[[Image:171031395_5877213677.jpg|250px|right]] '''Treat a lightning victim.'''  If someone is struck by lightning, call the emergency services immediately.  Determine if his or her heart is beating and if he or she is breathing.  Proceed with CPR or mouth-to-mouth resuscitation if warranted.  If the person is still exposed to possible lightning strike (yes, lightning can and does strike the same place twice) see if you can safely move the person to safety.  People struck by lightning do not retain an electrical charge, so do not be afraid of treating a victim.  If the victim has no pulse and if there is an automated external defibrillator (AED) available (many public places like sports arenas, grocery stores, etc. have them), send a bystander to retrieve it.  Continue CPR while the AED is en route.  Once it arrives, attach the AED and follow the voice prompts. i iz a baaadd man!"
                ]
            }
        },
