            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#[[Image:177385919_5a6d0da487.jpg|200px|right]] '''Know the signs of an imminent lightning strike.'''  If lightning is about to strike you or strike near you, your hair may stand on end, or you may feel a tingling in your skin.  Light metal objects may vibrate, and you may hear a crackling sound or \"kee kee\" sound.  If you detect any of these signals, assume the lightning crouch immediately."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#[[Image:177385919_5a6d0da487.jpg|200px|right]] '''Know the signs of an imminent lightning strike.'''  If lightning is about to strike you or strike near you, your hair may stand on end, or you may feel a tingling in your skin.  Light metal objects may vibrate, and you may hear a crackling sound or “kee kee sound.  If you detect any of these signals, assume the lightning crouch immediately."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "566756",
            "user": "67.79.14.30",
            "timestamp": "22:38, 26 February 2007",
            "anon": true,
            "size": "(8,885 bytes)",
            "change": -6,
            "summary": "",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "deletion",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#[[Image:173258005_19c2984a53.jpg|250px|right]] '''Watch the skies.'''  When you’re out and about, watch the sky for signs of approaching thunderstorms, such as rain, darkening skies, or towering cumulonimbus clouds.  If you can anticipate lightning before the first strike, you can avoid being caught in a bad situation.  Note that lightning can, however, strike even in the absence of these indicators.",
                    "#'''Take last-resort measures if you’re stuck in a dangerous place.'''  If you’re in a group of people, spread out so there are several body lengths between each of you.  Assume the lightning crouch: squat down with your feet together, your head tucked to your chest or between your knees, and your hands covering your ears or flat against your knees.  This is a difficult position to hold and it by no means guarantees your safety. However, by making it easier for a lightning strike to flow over your body rather than through vital organs, you may be able to sustain a smaller injury from it.",
                    "#[[Image:177385919_5a6d0da487.jpg|200px|right]] '''Know the signs of an imminent lightning strike.'''  If lightning is about to strike you or strike near you, your hair may stand on end, or you may feel a tingling in your skin.  Light metal objects may vibrate, and you may hear a crackling sound or “kee kee sound.  If you detect any of these signals, assume the lightning crouch immediately."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#[[Image:173258005_19c2984a53.jpg|250px|right]] '''Watch the skies.'''  When you’re out and about, watch the sky for signs of approaching thunderstorms, such as rain, darkening skies, or towering cumulus clouds.  If you can anticipate lightning before the first strike, you can avoid being caught in a bad situation.  Note that lightning can, however, strike even in the absence of these indicators.",
                    "#'''Take “last-resort�? measures if you’re stuck in a dangerous place.'''  If you’re in a group of people, spread out so there are several body lengths between each of you.  Assume the lightning crouch: squat down with your feet together, your head tucked to your chest or between your knees, and your hands covering your ears or flat against your knees.  This is a difficult position to hold and it by no means guarantees your safety. However, by making it easier for a lightning strike to flow over your body rather than through vital organs, you may be able to sustain a smaller injury from it.",
                    "#[[Image:177385919_5a6d0da487.jpg|200px|right]] '''Know the signs of an imminent lightning strike.'''  If lightning is about to strike you or strike near you, your hair may stand on end, or you may feel a tingling in your skin.  Light metal objects may vibrate, and you may hear a crackling sound or “kee kee�? sound.  If you detect any of these signals, assume the lightning crouch immediately."
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "id": "566124",
            "user": "74.100.83.167",
            "timestamp": "15:39, 26 February 2007",
            "anon": true,
            "size": "(8,891 bytes)",
            "change": -139,
            "summary": "",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "deletion",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "*Commercial lightning-detection devices should only be used as a backup to your own senses, such as in low visibility or high noise conditions.  Regardless of the readings you may receive on such a device."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "",
                    "*Commercial lightning-detection devices should only be used as a backup to your own senses, such as in low visibility or high noise conditions.  Regardless of the readings you may receive on such a device, seek shelter when the time between lightning and thunder is 30 seconds or less, and stay inside at least 30 minutes after the last flash."
                ]
            }
        },
